thank you all this is one of the last  photographs I have of my grandfather  I've taken it on his 87th birthday about  seven months before he passed away he  had Alzheimer's and in this photograph  he was five and a half years into his  battle with the disease  this photo reminds me of everything he  was an optimist a fighter to the very  end but this photo also reminds me of my  family's journey the many bad days in  the occasional good day when it started  with misplaced keys and frantic phone  calls eventually gave way to personality  changes aggression depression and  eventually loss of all function my dad  and his brother looked after Baba  my grandfather through all of his  illness but eventually it became so  overwhelming for them that we needed a  full-time caregiver nothing had prepared  them for the physical and emotional  demands of caregiving even though I work  in the healthcare industry I doubt I  would have been any more ready in their  situation you see as my grandfather was  going through his illness I look for  solutions that could ease the caregiving  burden on my dad and my uncle and I  could improve the quality of my  grandfather's life has Alzheimer's  ravaged his brain but I found very  little mostly informational websites a  few support groups and some products  that don't really work do you worry  about caring for an aging parent or  grandparent I do too and working in the  health care industry  I worry a little bit more because I know  that we face a much greater challenge  than our parents did and I also know  that the current health care system  unless changed will fall short of what  we need you and I are going to be the  caregiver generation and we will need  better solutions so today I want to  explore what we hear in the GSB  community future investors entrepreneurs  executives can do to make a difference  to this issue that matters to all of us  let me start with a few numbers we have  40 million people over the age of 65 in  the u.s. today they form 13% of our  population or 1 in 8 by 2050 they will  form 20% of our population or one in  five today we have five taxpayers to pay  for the care of every senior in the  country by 2050 we will have only three  2050 is not that far it's only 35 years  away and we will experience the impact  of these numbers in our lifetimes not  only will be face a greater share of the  cost of care we will also face a greater  burden and here's why people are living  longer the average life expectancy is 78  years today and is inching up into the  right into the 80s the fastest-growing  segment of our population today is  people over the age of 85 fewer people  today die from heart disease and stroke  but the prevalence of chronic conditions  such as cancers and diabetes remains  high and dementia  which is linked to age is expected to  become an epidemic and in fact it's set  to become the sixth leading cause of  death among seniors surpassing diabetes  the health care system was designed for  acute care and procedures not to take  care of people with chronic conditions  over a long period of time and in fact  about 40 million seniors only 2 million  live in assisted care facilities and  skilled nursing homes 38 million live in  the community in their homes and that's  where we will need to care for them as  you all know Medicare reimbursements  have been declining but what you  probably didn't know is that the cost of  uncompensated care or the care provided  by families you and I accounts for four  hundred and fifty billion dollars on an  annual basis and lasts on average eight  years we should expect those numbers to  increase and this is not just a US only  issue countries like China Japan and  even India that people consider to be a  young country also faceless challenge  for instance today in India those over  the age of sixty two five  the elderly account for 8% of the  population by 2050 that will be 20% or  323 million people more than the current  population of the US this is a real  problem and it's a big one and a global  one some of this will require national  policy changes and it will require  medical advances but I see a real  opportunity for all of us here at the  GSB and today I want to talk about two  opportunities in particular product  innovation and reimagining extreme users  let's start with product innovation and  no I'm not talking about incontinence  products and walkers what I am talking  about is smart solutions that help  seniors live healthier more independent  lives for longer connected to the people  that they love in their homes technology  things that we do so well here in the  valley  sensors wearables mobile touchscreen  have such a tremendous potential to  create better tools for seniors and  their caregivers and let me show you a  few examples of what I'm talking about  lift labs us-rok Health startup created  lifts where a spoon for people with  Parkinson's disease it works just like  your iPhone camera it detects shake and  cancel said and allows people with  Parkinson's to feed themselves here's  another example the beef shoe I don't  know how many of you knew Falls  yes Falls are a 30 billion dollar  problem in the u.s. Falls happen when  our footprint moves out of our center of  gravity while you and I can self-correct  Berkeley seniors can't and so they fall  and they fall more often but when they  do fall the consequences are often  catastrophic between 15 and 20% of the  Falls lead to a pape a hip fracture and  50% of the people who suffer fit  fractures  died within a year it's staggering many  solutions have been tried to this  problem with little success the B shoe  is taking a novel approach with  accelerometers what they do is detective  fall that's about to take place and  force a senior to take a step backwards  helping them regain their balance before  they fall  and here's another example lift here on  a Stanford startup out of stamp out of  Steve blanks lean launchpad class they  created uber for seniors to help to help  seniors get to doctor's appointments the  grocery store and do other things that  make up their daily lives when they can  no longer drive so my point is you don't  have to be a health care expert a  medical doctor to be an entrepreneur in  this space and quite honestly there's  actually never been a better time to be  an entrepreneur in this space reducing  the cost of senior care which forms a  vast majority of the healthcare costs in  the u.s. is a national priority and the  key players the government the AARP  health insurers and health systems are  getting behind it I I don't know if you  all knew but this is leading to the  creation of an emerging and thriving  ecosystem of startups that are starting  to come into this space startup health  data shows that the number of startups  in the space increased 33 percent  between 2012 and 2013 and funding  increased sixty percent over the same  period the AARP estimates a twenty  billion dollar opportunity and that is  just in the next five years and only in  the US even if you choose not to be an  entrepreneur in this space I'd urge you  to still look at opportunities in this  segment which is an increasingly  important part of the consumer market  and this brings me to the second  opportunity I want to talk about  reimagining the extreme user Katie FICO  was speaking at Stanford not too long  ago is the co-founder of aging 2.0 a  network for entrepreneurs and investors  in the space and Katie said something  which was quite interesting she said if  you designed for seniors chances are  that your products will find a bigger  market if it's intuitive to them it will  be intuitive to many not too long before  my grandfather passed away I was  visiting him I was sitting by his  bedside looking at old photographs of us  scrolling through my iPad he was  watching me and at one moment he reached  over and took the iPad from my  and without my prompting him his finger  slid across the screen and for a moment  I saw his eyes light up as the memories  came back I saw a smile wash across his  face it was a magical moment I was so  shocked I couldn't imagine that in his  condition when he didn't know the day of  the week it was he could operate an iPad  with no instruction and that it made him  happy  Katy's words were true we are going to  be the caregiver generation and we will  need better solutions so this is what I  ask if you are a GSB student looking for  a startup idea I hope you will consider  opportunities in this space if you are  at a start-up or at a company I hope you  will look at the segment with a renewed  lens and if you're an investor I hope  you will fund ideas that can make a  difference to all of us but here is one  thing we can all do today call your  parents and your grandparents and tell  them that you care they will appreciate  that call let's go change lives the  lives of the people we love thank you
 

